<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span>
We follow the setup described in Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Is TabGFM able to improve performance as the number of subsampled tables increases? ‣ 5 Experiments" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, which extends the general experiment setup from Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Experiments" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Implementation details" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. In this experiment, we vary the number of subsampled tables and corresponding TabPFN models <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\{1,\dots,10\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\{1,\dots,10\}</annotation></semantics></math>. All results are reported without PCA preprocessing.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
Per-dataset and overall mean accuracies for selected values of <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\{0,1,4,7,10\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\{0,1,4,7,10\}</annotation></semantics></math> are reported in Table <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.3 Per-dataset results for ‣ Appendix A Additional results" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, while Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.2 Is TabGFM able to improve performance as the number of subsampled tables increases? ‣ 5 Experiments" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> visualizes the overall trend together with standard errors. Across datasets, accuracy generally increases with larger <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. The most substantial improvements occur when moving from <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=0</annotation></semantics></math> (only LinearGNN components) to <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=1</annotation></semantics></math>, where TabPFN models are first introduced. For datasets with a large number of classes, this threshold can shift to higher <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> (see Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Implementation details" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>). Beyond this point, performance gains remain consistent, though less pronounced, as <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> increases further.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Implementation details</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments can be run with a single L40S GPU (48GB VRAM) and 64GB of CPU memory.
Unless stated otherwise, all reported results are averages across five runs using random seeds 0, 1, 2, 3, and 4, respectively. Random seeds both influence the dataset split used and the models themselves. All standard errors reported are calculated based on the sample standard deviation of values obtained from each seed divided by the square root of 5.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TabGFM node-level embeddings.</span> For the GPSE embeddings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> (see section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Node-level encoding ‣ 4 The TabGFM framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>), we use the checkpoint trained on ChEML <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. For RandomWalkPE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> and LaplacianEigenvectorPE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, we pick <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=20</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TabPFN.</span> For all our experiments, we use the newest version of TabPFN at the time of writing, TabPFNv2. For each TabPFN model, we subsample 2500 random labelled rows and 400 columns. To account for the imbalance in the number of feature-encoding columns vs. the number of structure-encoding columns, 300 of 400 columns are sampled from feature encodings and 100 are sampled from structure encodings.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Since TabPFN natively supports at most ten classes, we adopt the error-correcting output code (ECOC) strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> suggested by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hollmann et al. [19]</a></cite>, which splits tasks with more than ten classes into <math id="A2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> subtasks of at most ten classes each. Our subsampling strategy is applied independently to each subtask and aggregated outputs form one predictor in the ensemble selection.
As the ECOC-strategy generally needs at least <math id="A2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil n_{\text{classes}}/9\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>classes</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil n_{\text{classes}}/9\rceil</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure coverage, we do not apply TabPFN models on the dataset, if <math id="A2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B&lt;\lceil n_{\text{classes}}/9\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>classes</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B&lt;\lceil n_{\text{classes}}/9\rceil</annotation></semantics></math> (and the dataset has more than 10 classes), relying on LinearGNNs with ensemble selection. For <math id="A2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, TabPFN models are used on all datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LinearGNNs.</span> The eight additional LinearGNNs in TabGFM are each based on one of the eight node-level encodings presented in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Node-level encoding ‣ 4 The TabGFM framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.
We normalize the outputs <math id="A2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{l}=(l_{c})_{c\in C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐥</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{l}=(l_{c})_{c\in C}</annotation></semantics></math> of the LinearGNNs before ensembling using the following proportional scaling, which maps the smallest logit to <math id="A2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{l}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐥</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{l}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbf{l}-\min_{c}l_{c}+\epsilon," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐥</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>c</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbf{l}-\min_{c}l_{c}+\epsilon,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\mathbf{l}^{\prime}}{\sum_{c}l^{\prime}_{c}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>𝐥</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>c</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\mathbf{l}^{\prime}}{\sum_{c}l^{\prime}_{c}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This ensures that ensemble models with low weights cannot overrule other ensemble members by predicting unbounded logits with high amplitude.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ensemble selection.</span> Our implementation of ensemble selection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> samples models with replacement, breaks ties (during the greedy selection) randomly and implements early stopping, i.e. it picks the first model configuration in the history that achieved the best possible (accuracy) score on the held-out predictions. We used <math id="A2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-fold cross-validation to generate held-out predictions for all labeled nodes <math id="A2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. For TabPFN-based models, we use two folds and five folds for LinearGNNs.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Principal component analysis.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Finkelshtein et al. [8]</a></cite> apply principal component analysis (PCA) to reduce the feature dimensionality of the full-Cora (to 2048), co-cs (to 2048), and co-physics (to 1024) datasets. To ensure comparability, we retain this preprocessing step for all baselines and for TabGFM in direct model-to-model evaluations. Importantly, TabGFM’s subsampling strategy allows it to handle high-dimensional input features without additional compression or computational cost. For completeness, Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Per-dataset results for ‣ Appendix A Additional results" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a> reports per-dataset mean accuracies obtained when omitting PCA preprocessing on the affected datasets.</p>
</div>
</section>
